          "label": "<p>A patient is admitted with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Which type of trans</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient is admitted with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Which type of transmission-based precaution must be added to standard precautions?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contact precautions with gown and gloves</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Contact precautions target organisms spread by direct or indirect surface contact (MRSA, VRE, C. difficile). TB bacilli are expelled as droplet nuclei &lt;5 µm that remain airborne — surface contact is not the primary route.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Droplet precautions with surgical mask at 1-metre distance</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Droplet precautions (surgical mask, 1 m spacing) are for large respiratory droplets &gt;5 µm that fall within 1 metre (e.g., influenza, S. aureus). M. tuberculosis particles are &lt;5 µm and remain suspended in air indefinitely — a surgical mask is insufficient.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Airborne precautions with N95 respirator and negative-pressure isolation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! TB spreads via airborne droplet nuclei (&lt;5 µm) that remain suspended and travel beyond 1 metre. Airborne precautions require an N95/FFP2 respirator, negative-pressure isolation room, and restricted room entry.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Standard precautions alone are sufficient for TB</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Standard precautions apply to ALL patients but cannot contain airborne organisms. The &lt;5 µm droplet nuclei of M. tuberculosis bypass surgical masks and settle on distant surfaces, necessitating dedicated airborne precautions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the transmission-based precautions table: Contact = surface pathogens (MRSA, VRE); Droplet = large particles >5 µm (influenza); Airborne = droplet nuclei <5 µm (TB, measles, varicella). Particle size determines the precaution level."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent — you correctly matched M. tuberculosis's airborne transmission (<5 µm droplet nuclei) to the appropriate precaution tier requiring N95 respirators and negative-pressure rooms."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
