          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During hospital water surveillance, which organism is used as the standard indic</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During hospital water surveillance, which organism is used as the standard indicator of faecal contamination of potable (drinking) water?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pseudomonas is monitored in specialised water systems (dialysis units, ICU water supplies, cooling systems) but is an environmental organism, not a faecal indicator for drinking water safety.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Escherichia coli</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! E. coli and total coliforms are the standard faecal indicator organisms for potable water. Their presence signals faecal contamination and the potential presence of enteric pathogens (Salmonella, Shigella, Vibrio) even if those pathogens are not directly cultured.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Staphylococcus aureus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>S. aureus indicates contamination from human skin and nasal flora (relevant for surface and air surveillance), not faecal contamination of water supplies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Legionella pneumophila</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Legionella is tested in hospital hot-water systems and cooling towers to prevent Legionnaires' disease. It is an environmental waterborne pathogen, not a faecal contamination indicator for drinking water quality.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle: a faecal indicator must be exclusively intestinal in origin so its presence reliably signals faecal contamination. E. coli fulfils this criterion. Pseudomonas and Legionella are environmental water organisms; S. aureus is a skin commensal."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — E. coli's exclusive intestinal habitat makes it the ideal faecal indicator. Detection methods include membrane filtration (0.45 µm filter onto selective agar) and the multiple-tube fermentation (MPN) technique."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
